      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "To describe the case of a previously healthy 56-year-old woman, who presented with acute, nontraumatic pain in the left side of the neck and mild dysphagia."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "We report the results of the physical examination, imaging studies, and clinical laboratory studies. In addition, we describe the patient's hospital course after surgical intervention."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "A patient who sought medical attention because of acute, nontraumatic neck pain and dysphagia was subsequently found to have acute extracapsular hemorrhage of a parathyroid adenoma. Computed tomography and magnetic resonance imaging studies revealed a mass effect beginning in the neck and extending into the mediastinum. Surgical exploration of the neck and histopathologic evaluation confirmed the diagnosis of spontaneous rupture of a parathyroid adenoma with associated hemorrhage."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "Extracapsular hemorrhage of a parathyroid adenoma is rare and necessitates a high index of clinical suspicion for diagnosis. This diagnosis should be considered in a patient who presents with acute anemia, hypercalcemia, and a neck mass."
        }
      ]},
